# Line endings — LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# ⭐ This is not a style preference, it is a correctness requirement. Four tests in this
# tree are TEXT-LEVEL guards: they `include_str!` a source file and assert on the shape of
# what they find, because the defect they hold is the ABSENCE or POSITION of a call, which
# no unit test can reach (`every_presence_observation_is_recorded`,
# `every_ui_locking_message_has_a_handler`, `the_ladder_filters_the_batch_…`,
# `probes_and_actions_agree_on_where_they_run`). `include_str!` embeds the bytes AS THEY SIT
# ON DISK.
#
# So with git's default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, those files arrive CRLF and a
# search for `"\n}\n"` finds nothing: the guard panics instead of guarding. That is exactly
# what happened — 225 tests green on Windows, one red, invisible to ubuntu-only CI for as
# long as CI was ubuntu-only.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on commit and checks out LF everywhere, which makes the
# bytes a Rust test reads the same on every machine. The `\r`-stripping in those tests stays
# as belt-and-braces: this file governs a fresh clone, not a working tree someone already
# has, nor a zip download.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries and images: never touched, or the content is corrupted.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary

# ⚠️ Captured tool output used as a test fixture is BYTE-EXACT evidence — it is what a real
# machine printed, and `parse_winget_list` slices it by fixed-width header offsets. Leave it
# exactly as captured: a normalisation here would silently move the columns the parser
# depends on.
tests/fixtures/** -text
